WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.850
Hi, Steve  Kaufmann here ...today,
I'm going to talk about French.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.150
I'm going to speak initially
in English and then in French.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.180
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.790
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.550
And if you follow me on a
podcast, please leave a comment.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:26.010
So French, French has things that
makes it easy to learn and things

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.600
that makes it difficult to learn.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.050
The biggest sort of factor that would
make French easy is that it shares

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.599
a lot of vocabulary with English and
English is very widely spoken, not

00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:43.980
only in English speaking countries,
but by people all around the world.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.769
Uh, I don't know, statistically, I
think something like 60% of words in

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:56.325
English are of French origin or at
least of Latin based language origin.

00:00:56.325 --> 00:01:02.265
But most of that comes from French
from the time of the Norman French

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.665
invasion of Britain in 1066.

00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:09.855
So if there are so many common words,
I mean the biggest obstacle to learning

00:01:09.855 --> 00:01:16.065
a language is vocabulary, and when
you listened to the French portion

00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:21.570
of this video you will see that
most of the words are identifiable

00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:24.855
as words that we use in English.

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:31.785
And, uh, the French portion of this
video will be made available, uh, in

00:01:31.785 --> 00:01:35.835
LingQ as a lesson, even though I'm not
a native speaker so that you can look

00:01:35.835 --> 00:01:37.815
up words and phrases and learn them.

00:01:37.845 --> 00:01:41.235
So the big advantage, therefore
that English speakers have in

00:01:41.235 --> 00:01:42.945
learning French is vocabulary.

00:01:43.365 --> 00:01:47.085
I can tell you as someone who has
learned languages where there is little

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:52.710
or no vocabulary common to languages
that I know, it's a lot more difficult.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.290
And even though there are some so-called
false friends, the similarity in

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.600
vocabulary is an enormous advantage.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:06.060
Sometimes we don't recognize
that these words are the same or

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.880
similar to English words because
they're are spelled differently.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:15.180
Um, for example, wherever in
France, they use an, an accent.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:19.970
You know, the aigu and or the
circonflexe very often that hides

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.680
an 's' so ... is like study.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.040
Uh ... is school.

00:02:27.464 --> 00:02:34.545
Uh, ... is host or ... is hostile,
so that if you put an 's' in where

00:02:34.545 --> 00:02:39.614
you see those accents, you will find
it easier to recognize those words

00:02:39.614 --> 00:02:42.105
as being similar to English words.

00:02:43.065 --> 00:02:44.054
So that's the good news.

00:02:44.685 --> 00:02:53.054
What makes things a little more difficult
is that French spelling is not obvious.

00:02:53.114 --> 00:03:02.700
It's not obvious that EAULT is 'au'
um, you know, even the sound 'au' can

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.790
be as in ... meaning word or ... BEAU.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.405
In fact ... is written
MOT ... plurals is MOTS.

00:03:11.775 --> 00:03:12.925
And you don't hear the S.

00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:16.275
... BEAU.

00:03:16.535 --> 00:03:18.435
If it's plural, you put an X on it.

00:03:18.435 --> 00:03:21.315
You still don't hear the X,
unless there's a liaison.

00:03:21.615 --> 00:03:27.905
So that's just touching on the surface
of the difficulties with French

00:03:27.935 --> 00:03:33.125
spelling, not obvious to an English
speaker, but then spelling in English,

00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:35.855
isn't obvious to non-English speakers.

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:40.445
So this is something that we have to
get used to, and it is an element that

00:03:40.445 --> 00:03:42.455
makes French somewhat more difficult.

00:03:42.785 --> 00:03:48.185
The only solution is to do a lot of
reading and listening and getting

00:03:48.185 --> 00:03:53.075
used to how words that are written
a certain way are pronounced.

00:03:53.705 --> 00:03:55.145
Spelling, therefore, is a problem.

00:03:55.655 --> 00:04:00.870
Uh, I consider the issues of
pronunciation and comprehension related.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.330
Uh, some people find French difficult
to understand because of the nasal

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.850
sounds and because of the liaison.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:18.570
So first of all, the nasal sounds the
... don't exist in English and it's very

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.650
easy to get them wrong when we speak.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.890
And sometimes to not understand well,
when we hear them in different contexts.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.890
Uh, another issue that affects
comprehension is the liaison where

00:04:31.909 --> 00:04:37.169
very often the consonant of the
previous word almost seems like the

00:04:37.169 --> 00:04:39.450
initial consonant in the next word.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.560
And therefore we don't notice
what we're listening to.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:43.989
If you...

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:52.320
like ... if it's ... all by itself,
it's ... when it's followed by a vowel

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.170
and the next word, it becomes ... and it's
as if the cosonant has all of a sudden

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.680
been attached to the succeeding word.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:05.860
So those are things again that
we can read explanations of but

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.495
until we train our brain through a
lot of listening, it's difficult.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:13.155
Another difficulty in French
is gender and is not obvious.

00:05:13.575 --> 00:05:19.065
Whereas I say in Spanish words that end
in 'o' tend in most cases to be masculine.

00:05:19.424 --> 00:05:22.784
Words that end in 'a' tend
in most cases to be feminine.

00:05:23.385 --> 00:05:25.664
In French, it's less obvious.

00:05:26.054 --> 00:05:28.815
And I'll give you some examples
in the French portion of this.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.150
Uh, so gender is an issue.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:38.700
So ... plural is an issue, uh,
but all of these things, and I

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.540
should finish by saying verbs can
be difficult with many different

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.060
conjugations and a variety of tenses.

00:05:45.465 --> 00:05:50.235
Um, nowadays of course, with, uh,
conjugating dictionaries, such

00:05:50.235 --> 00:05:51.885
as we use it LingQ it's easy.

00:05:51.885 --> 00:05:58.365
Every time you see a verb to look
it up, see which form of the verb

00:05:58.395 --> 00:06:00.284
that is in the conjugation table.

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:05.775
And every time you do that and you kind
of scan the conjugation table itself,

00:06:06.015 --> 00:06:10.905
and it sort of helps you notice what's
happening, but in the end you simply have

00:06:10.905 --> 00:06:12.405
to spend a lot of time with the language.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:18.825
In the French video I will, uh, recommend
a song that you can listen to, to work on

00:06:18.825 --> 00:06:23.715
your pronunciation, uh, because this song
includes many of the sounds that I think

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:28.804
are crucial to French, such as the sound
of ... that is an 'e' without an accent.

00:06:29.084 --> 00:06:33.939
... when it's pronounced and when it's not
pronounced and it's called "Le Petit

00:06:33.939 --> 00:06:40.159
Cheval Blanc" so it's Le Petit Cheval
Blanc but can also be Le Petit  so

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:44.420
the 'eu' sometimes disappears, 'eu' is
sometimes pronounced at the end of the

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:48.920
word sometimes not pronounced, but I
think having a sense of the use of the

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:55.395
'eu' this is my personal theory is very
helpful in getting the feel of French

00:06:55.395 --> 00:06:57.135
and being able to pronounce French.

00:06:57.165 --> 00:07:02.354
And I very much recommend you listen
to this, um, song by Georges Brassens.

